--- !u!114 &-4647715318223461538
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "Since the probe placement is automatic, some of them may end up inside objects or behind inaccessible parts of the Scene. Probes that sample backfaces are considered invalid and they may cause light leaking which produces unwanted bright or dark patches on the geometries. An example of this light leaking can be seen in the image above. Note that the wall itself is not affected because it's lit using lightmaps.\n\nThanks to the <b>Dilation</b> feature, the system can replace the invalid probes with valid data from neighboring probes, to prevent unwanted light leaking.\n\n\nTo reproduce the issue in the image above, let's disable the <b>Dilation</b> and rebake the lighting.\n\n1. Add a Cube at the following location: <b>X:17 Y:2 Z:-2</b>\n\n2. Open <b>Window</b> > <b>Rendering</b> > <b>Lighting.</b>\n\n3. At the top of the <i>Lighting</i> window, select the <b>Probe Volumes</b> tab, and under <b>Probe Invalidity Settings,</b> uncheck <b>Enable Dilation.</b>\n\n4. Optionally, to speed up the rebaking of the lighting, choose a \"Draft\" or \"Medium\" <b>Lighting Settings Asset</b> under the <b>Scene</b> tab of the <i>Lighting</i> window. \n\n5. Click the <b>Generate Lighting</b> button at the bottom of the <i>Lighting</i> window to rebake the lighting.\n\nAfter several seconds, once the lighting has been rebaked, you will notice that invalid probes are now wrongly affecting the Cube, because the <b>Dilation</b> was turned off.\n\n6. <b>Enable Dilation</b> and generate the lighting again to get rid of the light leaking."
--- !u!114 &-4204253214428161942
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: 'To better understand the effect they have on the Scene, you can temporarily turn the Probe Volumes off:


      1. Disable them via <b>Edit</b> > <b>Project Settings</b> > <b>Graphics</b> > <b>Frame Settings</b> > <b>Camera</b> > <b>Lighting</b> > <b>Adaptive Probe Volumes.</b>


      Note that the dynamic objects in the third room will appear very bright, because they are not lit anymore by the local probes, but rather by the global probe derived from the Sky, which is very bright in this Scene.


      2. Re-enable the Probe Volumes by undoing the previous step.


      3. Enable the Probe Volumes debug visualization in <b>Window</b> > <b>Analysis</b> > <b>Rendering Debugger</b> > <b>Probe Volume</b> > <b>Probe Visualization</b> > <b>Display Probes.</b>


      As you explore the Scene, you will notice how probes outside the rooms are darker, and probes far from the visible areas have a sparser distribution.'
--- !u!114 &-3257226451532074404
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "We'll now cover indirect lighting sampling issues between probes having different spacing distances, also called <b>Subdivision Levels.</b> \n\nProbes are placed automatically using the <b>Min and Max Probe Spacing</b> properties in the <i>Lighting</i> window, under the Probe Volumes tab,  depending on the geometrical complexity. In this particular Scene, the spacing between Probes will fluctuate between different <b>Subdivision Levels</b> at 0.6 m, 1.8 m and 5.4 m. Empty areas may receive fewer probes, whereas geometrically dense areas may have more probes. \n\nWhile it may be necessary to reduce the total number of probes in the scene by increasing the <b>Probe Spacing</b> properties, some unwanted lighting seams may appear when sampling happens between different <b>Subdivision Levels,</b> as seen in the image above.\n\nTo prevent these unwanted indirect lighting seams in this scenario, we can use the <b>Sampling Noise</b> feature in the Volume system, in conjunction with <b>Temporal Anti Aliasing.</b>\n\n\nTo reproduce the artifact seen in the image above, follow these steps:\n\n1. Add a Cube at <b>X:5 Y:5.5 Z:0</b>\n\n2. In the <i>Hierarchy</i> window, select <b>Lighting</b> > <b>Volumes</b> > <b>Volume Room 1.</b>\n\n3. In the <i>Inspector</i> window, under <b>Probe Volume Options,</b> set the <b>Sampling Noise</b> to <b>0.</b> The indirect lighting seam will become obvious. As you can see, this feature is fully real-time, therefore it does not require the rebaking of the lighting.\n\nIf you do not see the <b>Sampling Noise</b> property in the <i>Inspector,</i> click the 3-dots menu on the Probe Volumes Options and enable \"Show Additional Properties\".\n\n4. Set the <b>Sampling Noise</b> back to <b>0.45.</b>"
--- !u!114 &-2839180831098631967
