--- !u!114 &2940801649425891655
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "In certain situations, some objects should be prevented from influencing the placement of probes. This is often the case for proxy geometries or distance objects. Indeed, these objects usually do not require more probes to be placed around them, as this would increase the baking time and the size of the Probe Volumes' data on disk.\n\nFor example, we have an object called <b>ShadowProxy</b> in the scene that is used to enforce correct light baking and real-time shadowing indoors. However, we do not need this object to be filled with probes, as they would be wastefully placed outside the playable area.\n\nTo prevent this object from getting probes generated around it, we have already used the <b>Layer Mask</b> system.\n\n\nTo understand how the <b>ShadowProxy</b> is excluded from the Probe Placement calculation, follow these steps:\n\n1. Enable the Probe Volumes debug visualization in <b>Window</b> > <b>Analysis</b> > <b>Rendering Debugger</b> > <b>Probe Volumes</b> > <b>Probe Visualization</b> > <b>Display Probes.</b>\n\n2. Take a look at the probe count currently visible on the screen. Unlike in the image above, probes do not fill entirely the space within <b>ShadowProxy.</b>\n\n3. In the <i>Hierarchy</i> window, select <b>Lighting</b> > <b>ShadowProxy.</b>\n\n4. At the top right of the <i>Inspector</i> window, you can see that the Layer is set arbitrarily to \"PostProcessing\". \n\n5. In the <i>Lighting</i> window, under the <b>Probe Volumes</b> tab, see that <b>Probe Placement</b> > <b>Renderer Filter Settings</b> > <b>Layer Mask</b> omits the \"PostProcessing\" layer.\n\nThis means that the <b>ShadowProxy</b> won't be part of the probe placement calculations.\n\n6. Optionally, if you wanted to force the <b>ShadowProxy</b> to be surrounded by probes, you would need to assign it to the \"Default\" Layer for instance, and click on <b>Generate Lighting</b> at the bottom of the <i>Lighting</i> window."
--- !u!114 &2980876633501624928
