--- !u!114 &5024033471480498815
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "<b>Probe Volumes,</b> also known as \"Adaptive Probe Volumes\" or \"APV\" for short, are a system that allows dynamic objects to receive indirect diffuse lighting. It shares the same goal as the <b>Light Probes Groups,</b> but has major differences. Some of them are listed below:\n\n\u2022 The probe placement is automatic and based on the Scene's geometric complexity: more probes will be placed around geometries, whereas empty areas of the Scene will receive fewer probes, depending on your <b>Min and Max Probe Spacing</b> settings. This automatic probe placement provides huge time-saving opportunities.\n\n\u2022 The indirect lighting is applied per-pixel instead of per-object. This allows to achieve much better lighting quality and, in some cases, Probe Volumes can replace lightmaps, notably when the Probe Spacing is lower than the corresponding Lightmap Resolution.\n\n\u2022 They can be used by the Reflection Probes to reduce indirect specular leaking. This is particularly useful to prevent unwanted reflections in darker or concave areas, without the need to place additional Reflection Probes in these areas.\n\n\u2022 They can affect Volumetric Fog and Screen Space Global Illumination.\n\n\u2022 Streaming functionalities are provided to support larger scenes automatically."
